on the left a former student protest  leader who in february turned 35 the  minimum age for running for president  why did we become used to so much  inequality why did we allow it to grow  before our eyes never again we will  change it  on the far right a second generation  german immigrant who supported chile's  former military dictatorship and  believes that there is a global  left-wing conspiracy  chile will never be a marxist or  communist country because we believe in  freedom  this is gabriel borich when he became a  congressman seven years ago for the  newly formed left-wing political  coalition that pushed for structural  social reforms  he represents a new generation that took  to the streets two years ago demanding  more equality in chile which has one of  the highest income inequalities in the  world his symbol is this tree from where  he used to imagine a better future for  chile from his home city of puntarenas  that overlooks the strait of magellan  agatha a single mother tells us she's  not worried about his youth  no i feel that's what we need a new  vision new ideas more support for young  people to get ahead  but some moderates and investors are  frightened of bodhichi's alliance with  the communist party  boris has changed his style to look a  bit more presidential and has been  recalibrating his program to reach out  to those who still see him as too  radical but he's not the only one who  has been significantly tweaking his  profile  former congressman cast is an old school  ultra-conservative catholic who opposes  abortion and same-sex marriage his motto  is make chili great again  he would expel undocumented migrants and  his position on climate change is  lukewarm  he would maintain chile's unbridled free  market model and lower taxes for big  business  but in recent weeks he's reconsidered  allowing the military to make arrests  without a warrant during a state of  emergency  cast's biggest drawing card is his  promise to restore law and order  what chilemos needs is security this was  a peaceful country where anyone could  walk around any hour now it's not safe  anywhere  cast represents the return of public  order and respect for authorities  bordich the hope that dreams of social  justice can come true  two different models confront each other  in this election two generations with  opposing visions of what chile needs two  different ways of seeing life as well  will we face convulsive times yes  either one could become the next  president of a country that has lost its  political center the very thing that for  three decades seemed to guarantee  chile's reputation as latin america  standard bearer of political and  economic stability lucia newman al  jazeera santiago
 

